    "c31": {
      "anchor": "hosting#markdown-2",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "11. Hosting",
      "excerpt": "People's accounts: the org makes one for each person who joins. It's how they si",
      "text": "table",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-02T03:57:54.813Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "People's accounts: the org makes one for each person who joins. It's how they sign in and read, and what they write goes in the org's repo.\nTheir names: people see ana@rs. Underneath, an AT Protocol name is a web address, so it's ana.rs.spell.spaces, or ana.their-own-domain.org for an org with its own. ana.spell.spaces is left for personal accounts.\nThe org's own host is a PDS, a Personal Data Server: the reference one asks for 1 GB of memory, 1 CPU, 20 GB of disk, a domain with a wildcard record, and a mail sender, and suggests that for 1 to 20 users.",
      "offset": 0,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-02T04:08:29.216Z",
          "html": "<p>Done:  a table of three rows.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-02T04:08:06.227Z"
      }
    },
